User: let's make it app configurable; some apps will use WoT-gate, some apps will only load from follows, some apps won't use that loader at all and only do the loading when the user explicitly searches for it instead of using the content renderer. It's not default on or off: it's whatever the app developer chooses to ship (just like all content parser NMP UI components that are up to the developer to choose whether to support, for example, to render naddr 30023 articles as a raw naddr or as a specific component for embedded articles.

Assistant: This is the right framing, and it cleanly dissolves the passive-vs-explicit deadlock in the second-opinion comment. Let me capture it precisely, because it changes the shape of the design: the framework stops making the on/off/WoT call and instead ships a **policy seam** that the app's composition root fills — exactly like component registration for kind:30023. [...] Your framing collapses the whole passive-vs-explicit deadlock the two comments were stuck on. The framework stops deciding **on/off/WoT** and instead ships a **policy seam** that the app fills at its composition root — structurally identical to how apps already choose whether to register a rich component for a content kind (raw `naddr` vs. embedded-article card).

**The seam:** the content renderer never hardcodes "when to fetch." Before it fires the AD resolver for a note, it consults a host-provided predicate:

```rust
trait AdResolutionPolicy {
    fn should_auto_resolve(&self, ctx: &AdRenderContext) -> bool; // pure, sync, no network
}
```

NMP ships built-ins; the app picks one or writes its own:
- `NeverAutoResolve` — content renderer never fetches; resolution happens only on explicit search/paste (your "only load when the user searches for it").
- `FollowsOnly` — auto-resolve only for notes authored by someone the user follows.
- `WebOfTrust { max_distance }` — auto-resolve within WoT distance N.
